# CP13 — charts and README. The first thing a judge reads.
#
# Part A. KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md written properly: the six entries that had been
# pointing at a section which did not exist since CP2, plus the measured
# negative results — withdrawn retry timing, 184 of 188 priors asserted, the
# calibration trade, the 4x flip, the auditor's simulation-only validation, and
# the three world bugs that each invalidated a headline before they were fixed.
# Every entry states the limitation AND its cost.
#
# Part B. Four charts, from `settle/eval/charts.py`, committed to out/charts/
# and regenerated by one command. Chart 3 shows the classes OURS loses, at the
# same weight as the one it wins, because a chart that shows only wins is a
# limitations section displaced into a picture.
#
# Part C. README in SPEC §19's fixed order, opening with a number, with the
# sourcing outcome and the calibration trade in the body.

settle/eval/charts.py
settle/eval/report.py
out/charts/
README.md
KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md
SPEC.md
PLAN.md
tests/test_charts.py
checkpoints/cp13.allowlist

# --- Two notes on where things landed --------------------------------------
#
# 1. `out/charts/metrics.json` is a data file living in an images directory.
#
#    `.gitignore` carries `out/*` with exactly one negation, `!out/charts/`,
#    and `.gitignore` is not on this allowlist. That is the only committed path
#    under `out/`, so the artefact every README number is checked against had to
#    go there. `out/metrics.json` is where it belongs. Moving it is a one-line
#    `.gitignore` change plus the two path constants in charts.py and report.py.
#
# 2. `report.py` and `charts.py` are two modules on purpose.
#
#    `report.py` runs five arms and reconciles each — two to three minutes. If
#    the charts rendered from a live simulation, CHT-1's determinism check would
#    need a simulation in the loop and the committed PNGs would not be
#    reproducible from committed data by anyone who cloned the repo. So the slow
#    step writes an artefact and the fast step draws it, and the seam is what
#    makes both testable.

# --- What CP13 found, recorded because it changes how the results read -------
#
# The entire OURS margin is one decline class. `auth_abandoned` goes to OURS by
# 48.9 points; it LOSES to B2 on three of six classes, including
# `time_shiftable`, which is 899 of the 2,000 cases. The aggregate 27.90% vs
# 25.65% hides that completely. This was not known before Part B, because
# nothing had ever broken the incremental rate down by class — the sweep varies
# priors and the headline table aggregates, and neither one asks where the money
# comes from.
#
# It is in the README results section rather than in Known Limitations, because
# it is a fact about the result and not a caveat on it. It also suggests the
# obvious next experiment: a hybrid that uses OURS on `auth_abandoned` and the
# ladder elsewhere would likely beat both, and we have not run it.
